  - [1mBrowser push[0m — reaches users with the tab closed. Needs a service worker, VAPID keys, and a permission prompt; also
    needs the in-app feed to exist first, since push notifications are fire-and-forget and users need somewhere to find
    what they missed.
  - [1mEmail[0m — reaches people not using the app at all. Needs a mail provider, deliverability handling, unsubscribe links,
    and digest batching to not be miserable. Largest add by a wide margin.

  [1mRecommendation: in-app only for v1.[0m Every other channel is a delivery adapter over the same event + subscription core,
  so building that core first costs nothing later — and picking email now means half this project becomes SMTP
  configuration.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which delivery channels should the first version support? → In-app feed (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: what counts as a change worth telling someone about. Two of these quietly pull in extra scope, so I'll mark
  them.

  The low-cost ones are edits to fields the task already has — assignment, status, due date. "Comment added" is
  different: it means building a comments feature, which is its own subsystem. "Any field edited" sounds generous but is
  how feeds become noise nobody reads — a typo fix in a description pinging six people teaches everyone to ignore the
  bell.
